  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 353 AM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nAn area of thunderstorms continues to fester from central KS into \nour north central KS zones early this morning, thanks to a LLJ \nsupporting maintenance of these storms. Since they have persisted in \nthe same general location for several hours, the environment is well \nworked over at this point, so not expecting severe activity with \nthese. Fortunately, rainfall rates have been slow enough to preclude \nflooding issues for the time being. As the nose of the LLJ pushes \nnorth, that should eventually move storms north into the predawn \nhours before dissipating. On the larger scale, sfc low pressure \nremains in southwest KS into the OK panhandle with an associated \nfront hanging around our area through today. Counties along the Hwy \n36 corridor look to have just enough cloud cover with the boundary \njust far enough south to keep temperatures from getting as hot as \nthey will be south of the front. As such, those northern counties \nhave a lower probability of reaching criteria to continue heat \nheadlines. Elsewhere, another day of oppressive heat is expected as \ntemperatures and dew points combine to bring heat indices into the \n105-110 degree range. The front doesn't appear to make meaningful \nprogress into Saturday, so opted to extend the Extreme Heat Warning \nwith similar conditions expected that day.\n\nRegarding storm chances, there may be enough convergence along the \nsfc boundary to develop scattered thunderstorms in the late \nafternoon to early evening both today and Saturday. Confidence in \ncoverage isn't all that high for tonight in particular, as CAM \nguidance hasn't been consistent either between models or from run to \nrun. Confidence in storms developing Saturday looks better, but with \nvarying ideas on placement between models. In any case, with little \nchange to the synoptic pattern and overall environment from the last \nseveral days, can't rule out a few storms producing severe winds.\n\nRelief from the heat finally arrives Sunday as the front finally \npushes south of the area, and the ridge aloft flattens and places \nzonal flow over the area through mid-week. Sunday and Monday see \nhighs in the 80s with perhaps a few low 90s -- much more manageable. \nTemperatures are forecast to warm slightly towards the middle of the \nweek with southerly low-level flow returning, but ensembles also \nshow widening ranges towards the end of the forecast period. This is \nlikely due to perturbations aloft bringing more chances for rain \nwith another front in the region.",
